<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Anorexia nervosa, restricting type, moderate (F50.011)</content>. The presenting weight loss, fatigue, swelling and dizziness now have their diagnosis. Dr. Haddad-Lindström confirms anorexia nervosa, restricting type, moderate by DSM-5-TR (BMI 16.0 at intake), long-standing, with compulsive exercise. Medical causes are excluded: TSH 2.2 mIU/L, CRP 1.0 mg/L, tissue transglutaminase IgA negative, glucose low rather than high, and normal sodium and potassium. Phosphate has fallen to 2.3 mg/dL after a week of the meal plan, an early refeeding effect; potassium 3.7 mmol/L, magnesium 1.8 mg/dL and glucose 74 mg/dL are acceptable. Pulse 52 with an orthostatic rise of 16, blood pressure 100/64 lying and temperature 36.1 C do not meet the thresholds for admission. The slightly worse ankle edema is the expected refeeding edema, not heart failure. Outpatient treatment continues with close monitoring. [S]<list><item>Phosphorus 250 mg twice daily with meals for three weeks.</item><item>Finish the 14 days of thiamine.</item><item>The dietitian will increase the plan by about 200 to 300 kcal every few days toward weight gain of 0.5 to 1 kg a week.</item><item>Weekly CBT-E with open weighing in the session; stop weighing at home.</item><item>No gymnastics and walks of no more than an hour a day for now.</item><item>Electrolytes, phosphate and magnesium each week for the next three weeks at the laboratory nearest to where she is.</item><item>Raise the feet for the ankle swelling; no diuretic.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Recurrent depression with persistent grief (F33.1)</content>. Passive thoughts of death on a few days, no intent or plan. Dr. Haddad-Lindström advises waiting until nutrition is under way and the ECG is repeated before starting an antidepressant, and beginning grief-focused work within therapy. [S]<list><item>Safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline, the clinic number and local emergency numbers abroad confirmed.</item><item>Antidepressant decision at the four-week review.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI (open weighing in the consulting room)</td><td>Weekly at first, then every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>47.9 kg, BMI 16.2; up 0.5 kg in a week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Orthostatic pulse and blood pressure, and temperature</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pulse 52 lying, rising to 68 standing; blood pressure 100/64 lying, 90/60 standing; temperature 36.1 C; dizzy on standing; below the admission thresholds</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium, potassium, phosphate, magnesium and glucose</td><td>Baseline; three to four days after nutrition starts; weekly for the first weeks; then at each visit while underweight</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sodium 137, potassium 3.7 mmol/L, phosphate 2.3 mg/dL (low), magnesium 1.8 mg/dL, glucose 74 mg/dL, seven days after the meal plan started. Protocol deviation: the check due three to four days after the meal plan started was not drawn, because she was at a spa and no laboratory had been arranged there. Phosphate had fallen by the time it was drawn, without symptoms or arrhythmia; it is being replaced, so the harm was limited to a delayed start of replacement. Corrective action: weekly draws booked in advance at a laboratory wherever she is staying, with results sent to the clinic</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Thiamine before and during early refeeding</td><td>Daily for the first 14 days</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taken daily since intake; to finish next week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Refeeding edema and signs of heart failure</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>1+ ankle edema, slightly more; no breathlessness, raised neck veins or crackles</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>ECG for bradycardia and QTc</td><td>Baseline; repeat with abnormal electrolytes and before QT-prolonging drugs</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Sinus bradycardia 52 and QTc 438 ms at intake; repeat at four weeks before any antidepressant</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Eating disorders psychiatry consultation</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Haddad-Lindström today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise and compulsive activity</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Walking down from about five to two hours a day; no gymnastics; new limit of an hour a day</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Multidisciplinary treatment: CBT-E attendance, dietitian, psychiatry</td><td>Weekly therapy; dietitian every two weeks</td><td>Ordered</td><td>CBT-E and dietitian booked, in person or by video</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>DXA of the spine and hip</td><td>Once for a postmenopausal woman with long-standing low weight; then every two years</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked for the four-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Passive thoughts of death on a few days since intake; no intent, plan or preparatory behavior; PHQ-9 16 at intake with item 9 scored 1. Protective factors unchanged. Safety plan reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>At least monthly during acute treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>16 at intake one week ago</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Medically stable for outpatient care: resting pulse 50 or more, no orthostatic dizziness, and normal potassium, phosphate, magnesium and glucose. In progress; target 2026-03-03 [S]</item><item>Weight gain of about 0.5 to 1 kg a week toward a body mass index of at least 18.5. In progress; target 2026-08-31 [S]</item><item>Depression response (PHQ-9 down by at least 50 percent) by six months. In progress; target 2026-06-30 [S]</item><item>Exercise reduced to walks of an hour or less a day while weight is restored, with no gymnastics until the team agrees. In progress; target 2026-04-28 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Eating Disorders, Fourth Edition (2023); Medical Management of Restrictive Eating Disorders in Adolescents and Young Adults (position paper) (2022); Eating Disorders: A Guide to Medical Care (AED Medical Care Standards Guide), 4th edition (2021); ASPEN Consensus Recommendations for Refeeding Syndrome (2020); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders and osteoporosis monographs (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Weekly blood tests. Review in three weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-03-03: four-week review: weight, vital signs, electrolytes, bone density, and depression. [S]</paragraph></text>

